  "solution": {
    "approach": "For each test case we scan the given string once. While scanning we maintain two counters: one for uppercase letters (characters between 'A' and 'Z') and one for lowercase letters (characters between 'a' and 'z'). After the scan we output the two counters. The algorithm is linear in the length of the string and uses only constant extra space.",
    "timeComplexity": "O(|S|) per test case, O(total length of all strings) overall.",
    "spaceComplexity": "O(1) auxiliary space.",
    "code": {
      "C": "",
      "C++": "",
      "Java": "import java.io.*;import java.util.*;public class Main{public static void main(String[] args)throws Exception{BufferedReader br=new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));int T=Integer.parseInt(br.readLine().trim());StringBuilder sb=new StringBuilder();for(int t=0;t<T;t++){String s=br.readLine().trim();int upper=0,lower=0;for(int i=0;i<s.length();i++){char c=s.charAt(i);if(c>='A'&&c<='Z')upper++;else if(c>='a'&&c<='z')lower++;}sb.append(upper).append(' ').append(lower);if(t!=T-1)sb.append('\\n');}System.out.print(sb.toString());}}",
      "Python3": "",
      "JavaScript": ""
    },
